    {
      "parameters": {
        "promptType": "define",
        "text": "=Your function is to take a meeting summary as input and convert it into actionable tickets with the specified schema.\n\nFrom the full meeting summary text:\n\n{{ $('Merge').item.json.summary }}\n\nextract all next actions. Compare them against existing tickets for the project, these will be titles and short summaries of each existing ticket:\n\n{{ JSON.stringify($json.tickets) }}\n\nIf any next actions are similar to the exisiting tickets then disregard them. If you are unsure whether to disregard or not then keep the new ticket, they will be draft tickets so they will go through manual review anyway.\n\nFor each ticket include a concise, descriptive name and a ticket body. Each section of the body should be have a h2 markdown header and the name of the section. An example is '## Summary'.\n\nThe ticket description should have the following structure:\n\n\"\n## 1. Summary\nA concise and descriptive summary of the ticket, used for comparing tickets to each other for similarity.\n---\n## 2. Problem Statement * **User Story / Objective:** As a [type of user], I want to [perform an action] so that [reach a specific benefit]. * **Current Behavior:** What happens right now (or what gap exists)? * **Expected Behavior:** What should happen once this ticket is resolved?\n---\n## 3. Scope & Implementation Boundaries * **In Scope:** \u00a0\u00a0* Specific requirement A \u00a0\u00a0* Specific requirement B * **Out of Scope (Explicit Non-Goals):** \u00a0\u00a0* Things specifically deferred to future tickets to prevent scope creep.\n---\n## 4. Acceptance Criteria (Definition of Done) Use Gherkin format (Given-When-Then) or clear, verifiable checkboxes:  - [ ] **Scenario 1:** \u00a0\u00a0* **Given:** A user is logged in with an expired token \u00a0\u00a0* **When:** They click the \"Checkout\" button \u00a0\u00a0* **Then:** They receive a refresh prompt without losing their cart items - [ ] **Unit / Integration Tests:** Coverage added or updated for new logic. - [ ]\n---\n## 5. Verification & Testing Steps 1. Navigate to `[URL / Screen]`. 2. Perform `[Specific Action with sample input]`. 3. Verify that `[Observable Result]` occurs.[11:23 AM]\n\"\n\nUse this framework to check that the ticket is an effective one.\n\nThe Ticket Readiness Framework: INVEST Before writing the ticket content, evaluate whether the item is ready to be worked on:  I \u2013 Independent: Can this ticket be completed without being blocked by an in-progress sibling task? N \u2013 Negotiable: Does it specify the goal and boundaries rather than over-prescribing implementation trivia? V \u2013 Valuable: Does completing this deliver clear functional or technical value? E \u2013 Estimable / Clear: Is the path forward clear enough that an engineer or designer knows how long it will take? S \u2013 Small (Right-Sized): Can this be completed within 1 to 3 days? If it takes a full sprint, break it down. T \u2013 Testable: Are there explicit criteria to prove that the work works?\n\nNever make up details, infer them strictly from the meeting summary. If there are no actionable items in the summary then output an empty JSON array. If a section defined here cannot be filled from what is provided in the meeting summary then leave it as an empty string.",
        "hasOutputParser": true,
        "messages": {
          "messageValues": []
        },
        "batching": {}
      },
